<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!-- TEST HARNESS ONLY — never leave this bootstrapped.

	     Same program as local.tank-snapshot, with three differences, all of them
	     about making a once-a-day job observable in one sitting:

	       1. All three tier intervals are 1 second and the slack is 0, so every
	          kickstart finds every enabled tier due. That is what makes the
	          retention/prune path reachable at all — on the real schedule it
	          takes eight days to accumulate a ninth weekly snapshot.
	       2. TANK_SNAPSHOT_PREFIX=test, so every snapshot this creates is named
	          test-<tier>-<stamp>. This is a safety property, not a convenience:
	          cleanup scoped to `test-*` cannot delete a production `auto-*`
	          snapshot no matter how wrong the cleanup command is, and the
	          production pruner's own `^auto-<tier>-[0-9]` anchor means it will
	          never touch these either. The two namespaces cannot collide.
	       3. Heartbeat interval 1, so the notification path gets exercised
	          instead of being skipped for the next 30 days.

	     WHY A WHOLE SECOND PLIST rather than env vars at test time:
	       sudo launchctl setenv TANK_SNAPSHOT_DAILY_SECS 1
	     fails with
	       150: Operation not permitted while System Integrity Protection is engaged
	     because it mutates launchd's global environment, which SIP forbids.
	     Per-job EnvironmentVariables is allowed. Measured 2026-08-22 while
	     building the scrub daemon; do not reach for setenv again.

	     THIS IS A FAITHFUL TEST OF THE PRODUCTION JOB, not an approximation. TCC
	     attributes device access to the binary being executed —
	     /usr/local/zfs/bin/zfs — not to the launchd label, so the Full Disk
	     Access context here is identical to local.tank-snapshot. Same uid, same
	     empty environment, same missing GUI session, same minimal PATH. That is
	     the whole point: a terminal run proves nothing, because sudo inherits the
	     terminal app's FDA grant, which is exactly how the boot-unlock bug hid
	     for three cold boots.

	     WHAT IT STILL DOES NOT PROVE, stated so it is not mistaken for covered:
	     with every interval at 1 second, this never exercises the "not due"
	     branch or the SLACK_SECS arithmetic, and because the tiers all fire in
	     the same run it cannot show a weekly outliving seven dailies. Kickstart
	     the PRODUCTION label once for the "not due" path — it will find nothing
	     due and exit 0 in under a second, which is itself the test.

	     Usage:
	       sudo launchctl bootstrap system /Library/LaunchDaemons/local.tank-snapshot-test.plist
	       sudo launchctl kickstart -k system/local.tank-snapshot-test
	       ... watch /var/log/tank-snapshot.log ...
	       sudo launchctl bootout system/local.tank-snapshot-test
	       sudo rm /Library/LaunchDaemons/local.tank-snapshot-test.plist
	     The last two lines are not optional. Leaving this loaded means an
	     unscheduled job that fires on the next `bootstrap` of the system domain.

	     Cleanup of what it created:
	       zfs list -H -t snapshot -o name -r tank | grep '@test-'
	       ... read that list, then ...
	       zfs list -H -t snapshot -o name -r tank | grep '@test-' | xargs -n1 sudo zfs destroy
	  -->

	<key>Label</key>
	<string>local.tank-snapshot-test</string>

	<key>ProgramArguments</key>
	<array>
		<string>/usr/local/sbin/tank-snapshot.sh</string>
	</array>

	<key>EnvironmentVariables</key>
	<dict>
		<key>TANK_SNAPSHOT_PREFIX</key>
		<string>test</string>
		<key>TANK_SNAPSHOT_DAILY_SECS</key>
		<string>1</string>
		<key>TANK_SNAPSHOT_WEEKLY_SECS</key>
		<string>1</string>
		<key>TANK_SNAPSHOT_MONTHLY_SECS</key>
		<string>1</string>
		<key>TANK_SNAPSHOT_SLACK_SECS</key>
		<string>0</string>
		<key>TANK_SNAPSHOT_HEARTBEAT_SECS</key>
		<string>1</string>
	</dict>

	<!-- No StartCalendarInterval on purpose: this job must only ever run when
	     kickstarted by hand. -->
	<key>RunAtLoad</key>
	<false/>
	<key>KeepAlive</key>
	<false/>

	<key>StandardOutPath</key>
	<string>/var/log/tank-snapshot.launchd.log</string>
	<key>StandardErrorPath</key>
	<string>/var/log/tank-snapshot.launchd.log</string>
</dict>
</plist>
